            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Measure your computer and monitor's actual draw with a plug-in meter or under load, then choose a UPS whose watt rating, not VA, is about twice that figure. A typical office PC drawing 200 watts suits an 850VA unit; a gaming PC drawing 500 watts under load needs a 1500VA unit rated for 900-1000 watts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a pure sine wave UPS?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a desktop computer with a modern power supply, yes. Most use active power factor correction, and some of those shut down or behave erratically on simulated sine wave. Routers, modems, monitors, TVs and older computers usually run fine on simulated sine. If in doubt, pure sine costs a little more and removes the risk."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a UPS run my computer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually minutes, not hours. A 1500VA tower UPS typically runs a gaming PC under load for single-digit minutes, around ten minutes at half load, and longer at light loads. Check the manufacturer's runtime chart at your actual wattage. The point is to bridge brief outages and allow a clean shutdown, not to keep working through long ones."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a UPS run on a generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, but line-interactive units sometimes reject generator power because its voltage and frequency wander, switching repeatedly to battery. Inverter generators produce cleaner power and usually work. Lowering the UPS's sensitivity setting often helps. For regular generator use, an online double-conversion UPS rebuilds the waveform and handles generator power reliably, as long as the generator has ample headroom."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a lithium UPS worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Lithium UPS units weigh less, last more cycles and keep their capacity longer than lead-acid, so they need fewer battery replacements. They cost more up front. For most homes, a lead-acid UPS for the computer plus a portable power station for longer runtime is cheaper and more capable than a single lithium UPS."
          }
        },
