        <div id="va-vs-watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. VA vs Watts: The Number That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every UPS is advertised by its VA rating, and almost every buyer reads that number as if it were watts. It is not. VA, or volt-amperes, measures apparent power, the product of voltage and current. Watts measure real power, the energy actually consumed. For a perfectly resistive load the two are the same, but for computers, monitors and other electronics they differ, and the UPS has a separate watt limit printed on its spec sheet that is always lower than the VA number.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why a 1500VA UPS is rated for 900 or 1000 watts, not 1500. The ratio between the two, called the power factor, varies by model. The CyberPower CP1500PFCLCD is rated at 1500VA and 1000W; the APC BR1500MS2 at 1500VA and 900W. Size your UPS against the watt rating. If your equipment draws 700 watts, a 1500VA unit rated for 900 watts has headroom; an 850VA unit rated for 510 watts will overload and shut off the moment the power drops.
          </p>

          <p>
            To find what your gear draws, use a plug-in power meter or the UPS's own load display once it is installed. Do not add up the nameplate ratings on power supplies, which describe maximums rather than typical use. A gaming PC with a 1000-watt power supply might draw 150 watts at the desktop and 500 watts under a heavy game. A home office PC with two monitors and a modem often sits between 100 and 250 watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aim to keep normal load at or below about half of the UPS's watt rating. Two reasons: runtime falls off steeply at high load, and battery capacity declines as the battery ages, so a unit that barely carries your load when new may not carry it in three years. A load around 40-60 percent of rating gives a usable runtime and room for the battery to wear without your computer shutting off unexpectedly.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your load is over about 800 watts, look beyond tower units. High-end gaming PCs with multiple graphics cards, or a workstation plus a large monitor array, belong on a 2000VA-or-larger unit, often a rack or tower model from the same brands. None of the picks here are sized for that, and buying a 1500VA unit to run a 900-watt load is setting yourself up for overload trips at the worst possible moment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sine-wave" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pure Sine vs Simulated Sine (and Active PFC)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Utility power is a smooth sine wave. On battery, a UPS has to build that waveform itself with an inverter, and cheaper units do it roughly: a stepped approximation that jumps between voltage levels, usually called simulated or modified sine wave. More expensive units build a smooth wave that closely matches the grid, called pure sine wave. While the power is on, both kinds pass utility power through unchanged; the difference only appears when the UPS is running on battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most modern computer power supplies use active power factor correction, or active PFC, a circuit that makes the supply draw current smoothly in step with the sine wave. It is common in power supplies rated for efficiency certifications, which today means most gaming and workstation PCs. Some active PFC supplies react badly to a stepped waveform. Symptoms range from a buzzing noise to the computer shutting off the instant the UPS switches to battery, which defeats the whole purpose of owning one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every active PFC supply has trouble with simulated sine, and plenty of people run one without issue. But you will not find out which camp yours is in until the power fails, and the price difference between a simulated and a pure sine 1500VA unit is modest. For any desktop with a modern power supply, buy pure sine. That means the CyberPower CP1500PFCLCD, the APC BR1500MS2 or, for lighter loads, the CyberPower CP850PFCLCD from this list.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Desktop PC with a modern power supply? Buy pure sine wave.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Simulated sine units are fine for routers, modems, monitors and most small electronics. For a gaming PC, workstation or anything with an active PFC power supply, the small extra cost of a pure sine UPS is cheap insurance against the computer shutting off at exactly the moment the UPS is supposed to save it.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For everything else, simulated sine is fine. Routers, modems, network switches, most laptop chargers, monitors, TVs and LED lighting generally run on it without trouble, because their power supplies are less sensitive to waveform. That is why the APC BE600M1 and CyberPower CP1500AVRLCD3 remain popular: they cost less per watt and suit network gear well. Just do not put them in charge of the one computer you most need to protect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="topologies" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Standby, Line-Interactive and Online UPS
          </h2>

          <p>
            UPS units come in three basic designs, and the names describe how much work the unit does while the power is good. A standby UPS, sometimes called offline, passes utility power straight through and only switches to its inverter when the power fails. It is the simplest and cheapest design, and the switchover takes a few milliseconds. The APC BE600M1 is a standby unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A line-interactive UPS adds automatic voltage regulation, usually shortened to AVR. When the incoming voltage sags or rises outside a set range, the unit uses a transformer to boost or trim it back to normal without touching the battery. That matters in homes where voltage dips whenever a big appliance starts, or during summer brownouts, because a standby unit would drain its battery on every dip while a line-interactive unit simply corrects it. Most desktop UPS units, including five of the six picks here, are line-interactive.
          </p>

          <p>
            An online, or double-conversion, UPS runs the load from its inverter all the time, converting incoming AC to DC and back again. There is no switchover at all, and the output is clean regardless of what the grid is doing. That is why servers, medical equipment and sensitive lab gear use them. The trade-offs are cost, noise from cooling fans and a little constant energy loss. For a home office they are usually overkill, though they are the best match for generator power, as the generator section explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Switchover time is the number that ties these together. Computer power supplies are designed to ride through very short interruptions, generally in the region of 16-17 milliseconds at full load under common ATX design guidance. Standby and line-interactive units switch well inside that window, which is why they work. Some devices that advertise UPS functionality, notably many portable power stations, quote longer or less specific switchover times, which matters if you plan to use one in place of a dedicated UPS.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a home office, a line-interactive pure sine unit is the right default. It handles brownouts without draining the battery, switches fast enough for any computer and costs a fraction of an online unit. Standby units make sense for network gear, where cost per outlet matters more than voltage correction, and online units for anything running off a generator.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Runtime: How Long a UPS Actually Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Runtime is where UPS marketing is least helpful. A box may quote a headline runtime at half load, at full load or at a load nobody actually runs. The only number that matters is the runtime at your wattage, which every major manufacturer publishes in a runtime chart or calculator on its website. Look up your actual load there before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough guide, the 1500VA tower units here use a pair of small sealed lead-acid batteries. Manufacturer charts for this class generally show single-digit minutes at full load, around ten minutes or so at half load, and something closer to an hour at around 100 watts. A 600VA standby unit running only a modem and router, which together often draw 15-30 watts, can last well over an hour. Runtime is not linear: halving the load more than doubles the runtime, because batteries deliver more total energy at lower discharge rates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery age is the other variable. Sealed lead-acid UPS batteries typically need replacing every three to five years, sooner in a hot room. Most UPS units run a periodic self-test and will warn you with a beep or an indicator when the battery is weak, and replacement cartridges are available for all the major models. A UPS with a dead battery still passes power through, so you will not notice it has failed until the moment it does not carry the load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lithium UPS models are increasingly available and address both problems. Lithium-ion or LFP batteries are lighter, last more cycles and hold their capacity for more years than lead-acid, which is why some network and small-office units now use them. They cost more up front. For a home user, the practical alternative is often not a lithium UPS but a portable power station with a UPS or pass-through mode, which offers far more runtime per dollar, with caveats covered below.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need more runtime than a tower UPS can offer, there are three honest options: buy a larger UPS with an external battery pack, put the UPS on a home battery or generator circuit, or move the long-runtime loads to a power station and use a small UPS only to bridge the switchover. Trying to stretch a 1500VA unit into an hours-long backup is a waste of money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="routers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. UPS for Routers and Modems
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most useful UPS in many homes is not on the computer at all. It is on the modem and router. Keeping the internet up through an outage means phones, laptops and smart home devices stay connected, a video call does not drop, and you can check the utility's outage map. Network equipment draws very little power, so even a small UPS runs it for a long time.
          </p>

          <p>
            A modem, router and small network switch together often draw 15-30 watts. A 600VA standby unit like the APC BE600M1 can carry that for an hour or more by manufacturer charts, and a 1500VA unit carrying only network gear can run for several hours. The catch is that your internet may still go down if the provider's equipment loses power. Cable and fiber networks have battery backup on their own equipment, but it does not always last as long as an extended outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have fiber, check where the optical network terminal, the box that converts the fiber signal, gets its power. Some installations include a separate battery backup for it; others plug it into a wall outlet that also needs to be on the UPS. Forgetting the ONT is the most common reason a UPS-backed router still loses internet when the power fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            For longer outages, small DC UPS units built specifically for routers, and power banks with DC output, can run network gear without converting to AC and back, which is more efficient. Our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> covers power banks with 12V output, and the <a href="/best-laptop-power-bank/">best laptop power bank</a> guide covers ones strong enough to run a laptop and a router together.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not overlook mesh nodes and smart home hubs. If your Wi-Fi uses several mesh units, only the one wired to the modem needs to be on the UPS for basic internet in that part of the house, but the others will drop out. Decide which rooms you actually need coverage in during an outage and back up those nodes, rather than trying to keep the whole network alive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-stations" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Power Stations, Generators and UPS Mode
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many portable power stations now advertise a UPS mode, sometimes called pass-through or EPS. The station charges from the wall and passes power to its outlets; when the grid drops, it switches to battery. Because a 1kWh station holds many times the energy of a tower UPS, it can run a home office or network gear for hours rather than minutes. For long outages, that makes it more useful than a traditional UPS.
          </p>

          <p>
            The question is switchover speed. Dedicated UPS units switch in a few milliseconds. Power station switchover times vary, and manufacturers quote figures ranging from under 10 milliseconds to around 30 milliseconds depending on the model. Anything at the slower end may not be fast enough for a desktop computer to ride through. For routers, laptops (which have their own battery) and lights, it does not matter. For a desktop you cannot afford to have reboot, a dedicated UPS between the power station and the computer is the safe arrangement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our guides to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> and the <a href="/best-ecoflow-power-station/">best EcoFlow power stations</a> note which models have a published UPS switchover time. A sensible home office setup is a power station plugged into the wall, a small pure sine UPS plugged into the station, and the computer on the UPS. The station supplies hours of energy; the UPS guarantees the instantaneous switchover. For whole-house versions of the same idea, see our <a href="/best-home-battery-backup/">home battery backup</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running a UPS from a generator is a common source of frustration. Many generators, especially conventional open-frame models, produce power whose voltage and frequency wander more than the grid does. A line-interactive UPS sees that as bad power and switches to battery, clicks back, switches again, and eventually drains itself. Inverter generators produce cleaner power and usually work better. Many UPS units also let you lower the sensitivity setting so they accept slightly rougher power, which often solves the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you run a home office from a generator regularly, an online double-conversion UPS is the real fix, because it rebuilds the waveform continuously regardless of input. Otherwise, use an inverter generator, lower the UPS sensitivity and make sure the generator is sized well above the load. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator for power outages</a> guide covers the inverter models that suit electronics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which UPS to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a gaming PC, workstation or any desktop with a modern power supply, buy the <strong>CyberPower CP1500PFCLCD</strong>. It is rated for 1000 watts with a pure sine wave output, which covers the large majority of single-GPU gaming rigs with their monitor and leaves room for the battery to age. If you prefer APC or want USB charging ports built in, the <strong>APC Back-UPS Pro BR1500MS2</strong> is the equivalent, with slightly fewer watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a home office PC drawing under about 300 watts, the <strong>CyberPower CP850PFCLCD</strong> gives the same clean pure sine output in a smaller box at a lower price. It is the sensible choice for a typical office desktop, a monitor and the network gear, and it fits under a desk more easily than the 1500VA towers.
          </p>

          <p>
            For routers, modems and network gear on their own, buy the <strong>APC Back-UPS BE600M1</strong>. It is inexpensive, compact and runs low-power network equipment for a long time. If you want more runtime for the network or have a mix of network gear, a TV and older electronics, the <strong>CyberPower CP1500AVRLCD3</strong> gives 900 watts and a bigger battery at a lower price than pure sine models, just not for an active PFC computer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The <strong>Tripp Lite SMART1500LCDT</strong> is worth considering if your workplace already standardizes on Tripp Lite or Eaton, or you find it at a good price, but for a home buyer the CyberPower and APC picks above cover the same ground with clearer waveform choices. And if what you really need is hours of runtime rather than minutes, skip the bigger UPS and look at a power station, with a small UPS in front of the computer if it needs a guaranteed instant switchover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by VA instead of watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1500VA UPS is not a 1500-watt UPS. It is rated for 900 or 1000 watts, and that is the number that decides whether it carries your load. Measure what your equipment actually draws and keep it at or below about half the watt rating, leaving room for the battery to age.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying simulated sine for a gaming PC</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many modern computer power supplies use active PFC, and some shut off or misbehave on a simulated sine wave. That means the computer can crash at the exact moment the UPS switches to battery. For any desktop with a recent power supply, pay the modest extra for a pure sine wave unit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging a laser printer into the battery outlets</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Laser printers draw large surges when their fuser heats up, which can overload a UPS and shut everything off when it switches to battery. Put printers, space heaters and other high-draw devices on the surge-only outlets, and keep the battery outlets for the computer, monitor and network gear.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never installing the shutdown software</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A UPS without its USB cable and software connected will run until the battery is flat and then cut power, crashing the computer anyway. Connect the data cable and install the maker's software so the computer saves work and shuts down cleanly when the battery is low, even when nobody is home.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the replacement battery warning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealed lead-acid UPS batteries typically last three to five years. A UPS with a worn battery still passes power through, so it looks fine until the next outage, when it drops the load instantly. Replace the battery when the self-test warns you, and note the install date on the unit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size UPS do I need for my computer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure your computer and monitor's actual draw with a plug-in meter or under load, then choose a UPS whose watt rating, not VA, is about twice that figure. A typical office PC drawing 200 watts suits an 850VA unit; a gaming PC drawing 500 watts under load needs a 1500VA unit rated for 900-1000 watts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a pure sine wave UPS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a desktop computer with a modern power supply, yes. Most use active power factor correction, and some of those shut down or behave erratically on simulated sine wave. Routers, modems, monitors, TVs and older computers usually run fine on simulated sine. If in doubt, pure sine costs a little more and removes the risk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a UPS run my computer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually minutes, not hours. A 1500VA tower UPS typically runs a gaming PC under load for single-digit minutes, around ten minutes at half load, and longer at light loads. Check the manufacturer's runtime chart at your actual wattage. The point is to bridge brief outages and allow a clean shutdown, not to keep working through long ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a UPS run on a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but line-interactive units sometimes reject generator power because its voltage and frequency wander, switching repeatedly to battery. Inverter generators produce cleaner power and usually work. Lowering the UPS's sensitivity setting often helps. For regular generator use, an online double-conversion UPS rebuilds the waveform and handles generator power reliably, as long as the generator has ample headroom.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a lithium UPS worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lithium UPS units weigh less, last more cycles and keep their capacity longer than lead-acid, so they need fewer battery replacements. They cost more up front. For most homes, a lead-acid UPS for the computer plus a portable power station for longer runtime is cheaper and more capable than a single lithium UPS.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station replace a UPS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For routers, laptops and lights, often yes, because they tolerate a short switchover and the station provides hours of runtime. For a desktop computer, check the station's published switchover time. If it is longer than about 10-20 milliseconds or not stated, put a small UPS between the station and the computer to guarantee an instant changeover.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I replace a UPS battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every three to five years for sealed lead-acid batteries, and sooner in hot rooms. Most units run a self-test and warn you with an alarm or indicator when the battery is weak. Replacement cartridges are available for the major models, and replacing the battery is usually much cheaper than buying a new UPS.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
